        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which kind of stud finder is the most reliable?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For drywall, a multi-sensor array like the Franklin ProSensor 710+ is the most reliable for most people, because it reads the full stud width at once and needs no calibration. On lath and plaster, a strong magnet that finds fastener heads is often more dependable than any electronic tool. Confirm either one with a small test hole before hanging something heavy."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my stud finder beep everywhere?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usual causes are calibrating over a stud, moving too fast, tilting the tool, a weak battery, or a dense wall such as plaster, thick texture or tile. Foil-backed insulation and damp walls also confuse capacitive sensors. Replace the battery, start away from any framing, slide slowly, and if the wall is plaster, switch to a magnet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is standard stud spacing in a house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most homes use 16 inches from the center of one stud to the center of the next. Some newer homes, garages and outbuildings use 24 inches, and older houses can vary. Measuring from a confirmed stud, a corner or the side of an outlet box gives a good prediction that you then check with the finder."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a stud finder warn me about electrical wires?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Certain models can. Tools with an AC detection mode, such as the Zircon X85 and Bosch GMS 120, alert to live, unshielded wires near the surface. They may miss cables in metal conduit, armored cable, and any circuit that is switched off. Treat the warning as a helpful extra and still avoid drilling directly above or below boxes."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do stud finders work through tile?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Often poorly. Tile plus mortar plus backer board is thicker and denser than drywall, which pushes many capacitive tools past their depth. A deep-scan mode helps, as does a magnet to locate fasteners in the backer board. In bathrooms, check the grout lines near fixtures for screw heads, and consider a radar imager for planning bigger jobs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are magnetic stud finders worth buying?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, as a backup and as the main tool on plaster. They need no batteries, never need calibrating, and stick to the wall so you can mark hands-free. Their limit is that they find fasteners rather than stud edges, so you still need to locate the center. At roughly $8 to $15, they earn a drawer spot."
          }
        },
