          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Stud Finders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are based on manufacturer specifications such as sensing depth, sensor count, detection modes and calibration requirements, plus how each tool suits drywall, plaster and specialty walls and recurring themes in owner feedback. We weighted reliability on the walls most homes actually have. No ranking is based on measurements we did not take.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Stud Finders Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most electronic stud finders measure capacitance, the way the wall stores an electric field. A stud behind drywall makes that section of wall denser than the hollow cavity beside it, and the tool registers the change. A <strong>single-sensor</strong> finder compares what it sees to a baseline taken when you press the button, which is why calibrating over a stud gives a false reading and why sliding too fast causes misses.
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>multi-sensor array</strong>, the design Franklin popularized, uses a row of sensors that each read their own patch of wall at the same moment. The tool compares them against each other instead of against a stored baseline, so there is no calibration step and the result appears as a band of lights covering the full stud width. That is easier to interpret and much harder to get wrong.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Magnetic</strong> finders skip electronics and grab the steel fasteners holding the wall to the framing. Because every stud has a vertical line of screws or nails, finding two of them in a column identifies the stud. <strong>Radar imagers</strong> such as Walabot send radio signals into the wall and build an image of the reflections, which lets them show pipes, cables and studs as distinct shapes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each design has a blind spot. Capacitive tools get confused by dense plaster, wet walls, foil-backed insulation and wall coverings like thick textured paint. Magnets are slow where fasteners are sparse. Radar costs much more and needs interpretation. Knowing the blind spot of the tool in your hand is most of the skill.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sensing depth on the box is a best case. A maker that lists three-quarters of an inch for standard mode and an inch and a half for deep mode is describing a dry, uniform wall. Real walls with several coats of paint, a skim coat of compound or a layer of wallpaper read shallower. If your wall is thicker than normal, pick a tool whose rated depth comfortably exceeds it rather than one that only just reaches.
          </p>

